<h1>Video 079 — try-with-resources and multi-catch</h1>
<h2>Video info</h2>
<p>Title: Core Java with OCJP/SCJP: Exception Handling Part-9 || try with resources and multi-catch block</p>
<p>Source: real YouTube auto-captions (.transcripts/079-0n4Rh2Anlbw.txt). ASR decoded in place: keep subing / keep supporting = keep writing (board); Bufford radar / buffer radar / perer radar / buer rador / Prof = BufferedReader; file radar / file R = FileReader; fileer / file writer FW = FileWriter; input. EXT / XT / exting = input.txt; I exception / IIO = IOException; APA / AP / jpa developer = API developer; perc of / par of / percent of = parseInt; yeser / set of / s of X / assert of = assert; ARR index = ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException; no class def of found = NoClassDefFoundError; exception in initializer = ExceptionInInitializerError; Tri / TR / Dy block = try; compar / compile / left and right = compile-time error; color / callar = caller; Legion / leg / leged = handle; reability / rity = readability; Artic / arithmatic = ArithmeticException; print stas / print St / print stack = printStackTrace; sop / s of / SF = System.out.println; rised / Riser / raer = raised; formated = formatted; autoc closable = AutoCloseable; CH of = child of; t n = &quot;ten&quot;; in I = int i; ENT room = interview room; O valid = valid; hazed / hitzer / habited = habituated; ly code = lengthy code; rid rid = rigid; rebound = restart; illegal H = IllegalArgumentException / IllegalThreadStateException (by context); 1 12 10 = 1 to 10; bral null = br = null; V / PR = br; try with resources. Java = TryWithResources.java; multi catch block. Java = MultiCatchBlock.java; crck = comment; through new = throw new; read through ing = rethrowing; catcher finally = catch or finally; public wide close = public void close; L leg / analan / anal = listen / and then; some people Sun = Sun people; web.xml error page kept as he said it; sry = sorry; bch = B.Tech; wake up meod = wake up method; Cinema is going to be done (when talking about finally) = cleanup is going to be done.</p>
<p>This video is Exception Handling Part 9. The first ~32 minutes finish the top 10 exceptions from the morning: items 7–10 (IllegalArgumentException, NumberFormatException, IllegalStateException, AssertionError) plus the JVM vs programmatic summary. Then Java 1.7 two enhancements: try-with-resources and multi-catch. Last ~7 minutes: two extra words — exception propagation and rethrowing an exception. He does not teach suppressed exceptions, reverse close order of resources, or Java 9 existing-variable try-with-resources. Do not invent those.</p>
<h3>00:07 — Recap: top 10; six already covered</h3>
<p>In the morning they started top 10 exceptions. Six exception classes already covered: whether checked or unchecked, whether JVM exception or programmatic exception. Four more remain. Keep writing. Sixth is done; seventh.</p>
<h3>00:29 — Seventh: `IllegalArgumentException`</h3>
<p>IllegalArgumentException. Child class of `RuntimeException`, and hence unchecked (children of RuntimeException are unchecked).</p>
<pre class='code'>class IllegalArgumentExceptionIsUnchecked {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // BOARD:
        // IllegalArgumentException is the child class of RuntimeException
        // and hence it is unchecked
        System.out.println(&quot;IllegalArgumentException -&gt; RuntimeException -&gt; unchecked&quot;);
    }
}
// prints IllegalArgumentException -&gt; RuntimeException -&gt; unchecked</pre>
<h3>00:50 — When do we get it? Thread priority 1 to 10</h3>
<p>Make sure you have clear clarity. Very important. He takes a `Thread` object — they covered this example in the morning.</p>
<p>For every thread there is some priority. The valid range of thread priorities is 1 to 10.</p>
<pre class='code'>class ThreadPriorityRange {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Thread t = new Thread();
        // BOARD: the valid range of thread priorities is 1 to 10
        System.out.println(&quot;valid thread priorities: 1 to 10&quot;);
        t.setPriority(5); // inside 1..10 — he will contrast 7 vs 15 next
    }
}
// prints valid thread priorities: 1 to 10</pre>
<h3>01:30 — `setPriority(7)` valid; `setPriority(15)` illegal</h3>
<p>If we are trying to set the priority other than 1 to 10:</p>
<pre class='code'>class SetPrioritySeven {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Thread t = new Thread();
        t.setPriority(7); // valid — 7 is inside 1..10
    }
}
class SetPriorityFifteen {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Thread t = new Thread();
        t.setPriority(15); // 15 is other than 1 to 10
    }
}
// runtime: IllegalArgumentException</pre>
<p>Immediately we are going to get runtime exception saying `IllegalArgumentException`.</p>
<h3>02:01 — Legal vs illegal: programmer / API developer, not JVM</h3>
<p>Whether the argument is legal or illegal — who is responsible to decide? Either programmer or API developer. JVM doesn’t have that information. That’s why IllegalArgumentException is not a JVM exception. It is a programmatic exception. They discussed that in the morning.</p>
<h3>02:25 — Two board points for `IllegalArgumentException`</h3>
<p>Point 1: child class of RuntimeException and hence unchecked.</p>
<p>Point 2: raised explicitly either by programmer or by API developer to indicate that a method has been invoked with illegal argument.</p>
<p>Seven is the legal argument. Fifteen is the illegal argument. Observe the difference.</p>
<pre class='code'>class IllegalArgumentExceptionBoard {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // BOARD:
        // 1. child class of RuntimeException and hence unchecked
        // 2. raised explicitly either by programmer or by API developer
        //    to indicate that a method has been invoked with illegal argument
        System.out.println(&quot;raised explicitly: method invoked with illegal argument&quot;);
    }
}
// prints raised explicitly: method invoked with illegal argument</pre>
<h3>04:28 — Board dictation: valid range 1 to 10</h3>
<p>Keep writing. He repeats every fragment.</p>
<p>The valid range of thread priorities is 1 to 10. If we are trying to set the priority with any other value, then we will get runtime exception saying `IllegalArgumentException`.</p>
<h3>05:51 — Board example: `Thread t = new Thread()`</h3>
<p>Take this example.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Thread t = new Thread();
        t.setPriority(7);  // valid
        t.setPriority(15); // we will get runtime exception saying IllegalArgumentException
    }
}
// runtime: IllegalArgumentException</pre>
<p>He first writes t.setPriority(7) — valid or invalid? Valid. Then t.setPriority(15) — runtime exception saying IllegalArgumentException. That’s all. What you people should remember: what is IllegalArgumentException.</p>
<h3>06:56 — Eighth: `NumberFormatException`</h3>
<p>Keep writing: `NumberFormatException`. Observe a bit carefully.</p>
<h3>07:15 — `Integer.parseInt(&quot;10&quot;)` is valid</h3>
<pre class='code'>class ParseIntTenIsValid {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int i = Integer.parseInt(&quot;10&quot;); // valid — no problem at all
        System.out.println(i);
    }
}
// prints 10</pre>
<h3>07:47 — `Integer.parseInt(&quot;ten&quot;)` — string not a number</h3>
<p>Sometimes: int i = Integer.parseInt(&quot;ten&quot;). Valid or invalid?</p>
<p>We are trying to convert string to the number, but the string is not properly formatted. This string is not representing a number. Immediately we will get runtime exception saying `NumberFormatException`.</p>
<p>Take special care. Have you observed the word: string is not properly formatted, string is not representing a number — that’s why NumberFormatException.</p>
<pre class='code'>class ParseIntTenWord {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int i = Integer.parseInt(&quot;ten&quot;); // string is not representing a number
    }
}
// runtime: NumberFormatException</pre>
<h3>08:49 — `NumberFormatException` is a special case of `IllegalArgumentException`</h3>
<p>Think a bit high level. &quot;10&quot; is the legal argument. &quot;ten&quot; is the illegal argument. Can I use the term: is it IllegalArgumentException or not? For this method this one is illegal argument.</p>
<p>Don’t think NumberFormatException is a separate new thing. `NumberFormatException` is the special case of `IllegalArgumentException` only.</p>
<p>In the API: for IllegalArgumentException, child is `NumberFormatException`. He already specified this on the diagram: NumberFormatException is the child of IllegalArgumentException; IllegalArgumentException is the child of RuntimeException. Checked or unchecked? Unchecked.</p>
<pre class='code'>class NumberFormatIsSpecialIllegalArgument {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int legal = Integer.parseInt(&quot;10&quot;);   // legal argument
        int illegal = Integer.parseInt(&quot;ten&quot;); // illegal argument for parseInt
        // NumberFormatException is special case of IllegalArgumentException only
    }
}
// runtime: NumberFormatException</pre>
<h3>10:02 — Hierarchy recap (he will correct “direct child of RuntimeException”)</h3>
<p>NumberFormatException is child class of IllegalArgumentException, which is the child class of RuntimeException, and hence unchecked.</p>
<p>Second point: we are trying to convert string to number but the string is not properly formatted — then NumberFormatException. Explicitly raised by either programmer or API developer to indicate that we are trying to convert string to number but the string is not properly formatted.</p>
<h3>10:39 — Board: he starts “direct child of RuntimeException”, then sorry — wrong</h3>
<p>Keep writing. He starts: it is the direct child class of `RuntimeException`. Then: sorry, sorry, wrong. It is the direct child class of `IllegalArgumentException`, which is the child class of RuntimeException, and hence it is unchecked.</p>
<p>Raised explicitly either by programmer or by API developer to indicate that we are trying to convert string to number and the string is not properly formatted.</p>
<pre class='code'>class NumberFormatExceptionBoard {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // BOARD (after he corrects himself):
        // NumberFormatException is the direct child class of IllegalArgumentException
        // which is the child class of RuntimeException
        // and hence it is unchecked
        //
        // raised explicitly either by programmer or by API developer
        // to indicate that we are trying to convert string to number
        // and the string is not properly formatted
        System.out.println(&quot;NFE = child of IAE = child of RuntimeException = unchecked&quot;);
    }
}
// prints NFE = child of IAE = child of RuntimeException = unchecked</pre>
<h3>13:09 — Board example: `parseInt(&quot;10&quot;)` vs `parseInt(&quot;ten&quot;)`</h3>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int i = Integer.parseInt(&quot;10&quot;);  // completed — valid
        int j = Integer.parseInt(&quot;ten&quot;); // runtime exception saying NumberFormatException
    }
}
// runtime: NumberFormatException</pre>
<p>That’s all. He hopes it is clear.</p>
<h3>14:09 — Ninth: `IllegalStateException`</h3>
<p>Keep writing: `IllegalStateException`. Listen. What is legal state, which state is illegal state? Make sure you people should be aware.</p>
<h3>14:41 — Story: wake up on a sleeping person vs a dead person</h3>
<p>A person happily sleeping. He went there: can you please wake up, shall we go for cinema, shall we go for tea. Can I call wake up method on the sleeping person? Legal — no problem. He is going to wake up and say interested or not interested. Calling wake up on the sleeping person is appropriate, correct time.</p>
<p>Suppose a person is dead. He went there: Durga, can you please wake up, shall we go for cinema. Can I call wake up on the dead person? Illegal. We are calling wake up method at wrong time. It is not the appropriate time.</p>
<p>To indicate that a method has been invoked at the wrong time we have to raise `IllegalStateException`. There is not such a type of state. On the dead person if I call wake up, what is the next state? No state is defined. That’s why illegal state.</p>
<p>This story is not a Java type. The technical example he maps it to is `Thread.start()` twice (next).</p>
<h3>16:16 — Two points: unchecked; method invoked at wrong time</h3>
<p>IllegalStateException is the child class of `RuntimeException`. It is unchecked. Raised explicitly either by programmer or by API developer to indicate that a method has been invoked at a wrong time. Wrong time — for that, IllegalStateException will be raised.</p>
<pre class='code'>class IllegalStateExceptionBoard {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // BOARD:
        // child class of RuntimeException and hence unchecked
        // raised explicitly either by programmer or by API developer
        // to indicate that a method has been invoked at wrong time
        System.out.println(&quot;IllegalStateException: method invoked at wrong time&quot;);
    }
}
// prints IllegalStateException: method invoked at wrong time</pre>
<h3>16:42 — Technical example: thread born, `t.start()`, cannot restart (Brahma / Yama)</h3>
<p>He created one thread object. One kid just born — or one thread born. Thread life cycle / human being life cycle more or less same.</p>
<pre class='code'>class ThreadBorn {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Thread t = new Thread(); // one thread born
        t.start();               // start its life cycle — valid, no problem
    }
}</pre>
<p>t.start() — happily this person enjoying his life. Almost around 25 or 30 years came. Then this person realized he didn’t study 10th class properly — 35% marks — not getting the job even after B.Tech. He sent a mail to Brahma: I didn’t study 10th properly, can you please restart my life so that happily I can study 10th this time.</p>
<p>Brahma’s job: just to start your life. I’m not responsible if you kill your life and restart once again. Why don’t you contact Yama for this purpose. The kid sends a second mail to Brahma: I don’t know who is Yama, can you please restart my life once again. Immediately Brahma is going to raise runtime exception saying `IllegalStateException`.</p>
<p>Same style: t.start() — thread life cycle already started. Now I’m trying to restart the same thread once again.</p>
<h3>19:02 — Interview: after starting a thread, restart same thread</h3>
<p>Very important question for the interview room. After starting a thread, if we are trying to restart the same thread once again:</p>
<pre class='code'>class RestartSameThread {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Thread t = new Thread();
        t.start(); // first time — appropriate, no problem
        t.start(); // second time — inappropriate
    }
}
// runtime: IllegalThreadStateException</pre>
<p>Immediately we will get runtime exception saying `IllegalThreadStateException`. Of course `IllegalStateException` only — by default we are going to get `IllegalThreadStateException`.</p>
<p>Calling start first time is appropriate. Calling start second time is inappropriate. If we call a method at wrong time, which exception? `IllegalStateException`.</p>
<h3>20:02 — Board dictation: `IllegalStateException`</h3>
<p>Keep writing. Child class of RuntimeException and hence unchecked. Raised explicitly either by programmer or by API developer to indicate that a method has been invoked at wrong time.</p>
<h3>21:25 — Theory example on the board</h3>
<p>After starting of a thread we are not allowed to restart the same thread once again. Otherwise we will get runtime exception saying `IllegalThreadStateException`.</p>
<pre class='code'>class AfterStartCannotRestart {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // BOARD:
        // after starting of a thread we are not allowed to restart
        // the same thread once again
        // otherwise we will get runtime exception saying IllegalThreadStateException
        Thread t = new Thread();
        t.start();
        t.start();
    }
}
// runtime: IllegalThreadStateException</pre>
<h3>22:40 — Board program: `t.start()` then `t.start()` again</h3>
<p>Take this example first.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Thread t = new Thread();
        t.start(); // valid, no problem at all
        t.start(); // runtime exception saying IllegalThreadStateException
    }
}
// runtime: IllegalThreadStateException</pre>
<p>Make sure you people should be aware clearly.</p>
<h3>23:31 — Tenth: `AssertionError`</h3>
<p>Keep writing: `AssertionError`. In the assertions chapter he will explain much, but compulsory you have basic idea.</p>
<p>It is the child class of `Error`. Checked or unchecked? Unchecked.</p>
<p>Raised explicitly by the programmer or API developer to indicate that assert statement fails.</p>
<h3>24:05 — `assert x &gt; 10`</h3>
<p>Best example:</p>
<pre class='code'>class AssertXGreaterThan10 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int x = 5;
        assert x &gt; 10; // if everything goes fine, x should be greater than 10
    }
}
// runtime: AssertionError   (when assertions are enabled: java -ea)</pre>
<p>If everything goes fine at this point of time, x value should be greater than 10. If it is not greater than 10, something went wrong — raise immediately `AssertionError`. In assertions he will explain. If assert statement condition fails, then we will get AssertionError.</p>
<p>He also says “runtime exception saying AssertionError” while dictating the example. On the classification board he is clear: child of `Error`, unchecked.</p>
<h3>24:52 — Board dictation: `AssertionError`</h3>
<p>It is the child class of `Error` and hence it is unchecked. Raised explicitly by the programmer or by API developer to indicate that assert statement fails.</p>
<pre class='code'>class AssertionErrorBoard {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // BOARD:
        // AssertionError is the child class of Error
        // and hence it is unchecked
        // raised explicitly by the programmer or by API developer
        // to indicate that assert statement fails
        System.out.println(&quot;AssertionError -&gt; Error -&gt; unchecked&quot;);
    }
}
// prints AssertionError -&gt; Error -&gt; unchecked</pre>
<h3>26:06 — Board example: `assert x &gt; 10`</h3>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int x = 5;
        assert x &gt; 10;
        // if x is not greater than 10 then we will get runtime exception saying AssertionError
    }
}
// runtime: AssertionError   (java -ea)</pre>
<p>That’s all. These are various important conclusions related to assertions. Not required to keep much explanation now.</p>
<h3>27:09 — Summarize the top 10 exceptions</h3>
<p>These are the top 10 exceptions. Let me summarize all these. Observe: exception or error; raised by whom — JVM or programmer / API developer.</p>
<p>He asks the room to spell them one by one. First six they already covered in the morning:</p>
<ol><li>`ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException`</li></ol>
<ol><li>`NullPointerException`</li></ol>
<ol><li>`ClassCastException`</li></ol>
<ol><li>`StackOverflowError`</li></ol>
<ol><li>`NoClassDefFoundError`</li></ol>
<ol><li>`ExceptionInInitializerError`</li></ol>
<h3>27:45 — First six: raised automatically by JVM → JVM exceptions</h3>
<p>Listen. Up to this, whatever we covered: these things raised automatically by JVM, and hence these are JVM exceptions.</p>
<pre class='code'>class JvmExceptionsHeListed {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(&quot;ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException&quot;);
        System.out.println(&quot;NullPointerException&quot;);
        System.out.println(&quot;ClassCastException&quot;);
        System.out.println(&quot;StackOverflowError&quot;);
        System.out.println(&quot;NoClassDefFoundError&quot;);
        System.out.println(&quot;ExceptionInInitializerError&quot;);
        System.out.println(&quot;raised automatically by JVM =&gt; JVM exceptions&quot;);
    }
}
// prints ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException
// prints NullPointerException
// prints ClassCastException
// prints StackOverflowError
// prints NoClassDefFoundError
// prints ExceptionInInitializerError
// prints raised automatically by JVM =&gt; JVM exceptions</pre>
<h3>29:33 — Remaining four: raised explicitly → programmatic exceptions</h3>
<p>Seventh: `IllegalArgumentException`. Next: `NumberFormatException`. Next: `IllegalStateException`. Next: `AssertionError`.</p>
<p>These things raised explicitly either by programmer or by API developer. That’s why these are programmatic exceptions.</p>
<pre class='code'>class ProgrammaticExceptionsHeListed {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(&quot;IllegalArgumentException&quot;);
        System.out.println(&quot;NumberFormatException&quot;);
        System.out.println(&quot;IllegalStateException&quot;);
        System.out.println(&quot;AssertionError&quot;);
        System.out.println(&quot;raised explicitly by programmer or API developer =&gt; programmatic&quot;);
    }
}
// prints IllegalArgumentException
// prints NumberFormatException
// prints IllegalStateException
// prints AssertionError
// prints raised explicitly by programmer or API developer =&gt; programmatic</pre>
<h3>31:07 — Exam shape: which are JVM vs programmatic</h3>
<p>From this, what kind of question for the exam: spell out some four or five — which of the following are JVM exceptions or which of the following are programmatic exceptions or which are raised by the JVM or which are raised by API developer. You have to decide.</p>
<h3>31:52 — Trap: `ExceptionInInitializerError` is the new one</h3>
<p>The most dangerous thing — take very special care — is `ExceptionInInitializerError`. For most of the people it is new. Maximum chance to ask in the exam is this one. Take this part clearly.</p>
<p>That’s all related to top 10 exceptions.</p>
<h3>32:45 — Heading: 1.7 version enhancements w.r.t. exception handling</h3>
<p>Keep writing: 1.7 version enhancements with respect to exception handling.</p>
<p>In the 1.7 version there are two concepts as part of exception handling.</p>
<h3>33:28 — The two 1.7 concepts</h3>
<p>First: try with resources. Second: multi-catch block.</p>
<p>As a part of 1.7 version, in exception handling, the following two concepts introduced:</p>
<pre class='code'>class Java17ExceptionEnhancements {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // BOARD:
        // as a part of 1.7 version in exception handling
        // the following two concepts introduced
        // 1. try with resources
        // 2. multi catch block
        System.out.println(&quot;1. try-with-resources&quot;);
        System.out.println(&quot;2. multi-catch block&quot;);
    }
}
// prints 1. try-with-resources
// prints 2. multi-catch block</pre>
<h3>34:57 — Try with resources (heading)</h3>
<p>Keep writing: try with the resources. Simple. Listen well.</p>
<h3>35:09 — Until 1.6: whatever we open in try, close in finally</h3>
<p>Until 1.6 version, whatever resources we open as a part of try block, we have to close inside finally block.</p>
<h3>35:32 — Board: 1.6 `BufferedReader` + `finally` close</h3>
<p>Carefully. BufferedReader br = null. Try block: br = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(&quot;input.txt&quot;)). Created BufferedReader pointing to input.txt. By using this object happily we can read data present in input.txt. Use br based on our requirement.</p>
<p>There may be a chance of `IOException`. Catch: corresponding handling code. Then finally: if br is not equal to null (we are pointing to some BufferedReader object pointing to a file), then `br.close()`.</p>
<p>This is our style until 1.6 version. You won’t feel a problem because we are already habituated with this style. Strictly speaking there are some difficulties. He will show. More simpler code we can write. From programmer point of view this much lengthy code is not required.</p>
<pre class='code'>import java.io.*;

class Until16CloseInFinally {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
        BufferedReader br = null;
        try {
            br = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(&quot;input.txt&quot;));
            // use br based on our requirement
        } catch (IOException e) {
            // handling code
        } finally {
            if (br != null) {
                br.close();
            }
        }
    }
}</pre>
<h3>38:03 — Problems: programmer must close; finally is compulsory</h3>
<p>Whatever resources we opened as a part of try block, compulsory we should close inside finally block. Is it complexity / burden to the programmer or not? By mistake if I forgot I didn’t close — we are going to waste one resource.</p>
<ol><li>Compulsory we should close inside finally — burden, complexity of programming increased.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Compulsory we should write finally block — length of the code increased, readability is going to be down.</li></ol>
<p>You may not see that much difficulty because we are already habituated. In 1.7 a new concept came: try with resources. Once you see the new syntax, what problem was there with the old — you can get.</p>
<h3>39:05 — 1.7 advantage: resources closed automatically</h3>
<p>Same code in 1.7 try-with-resources. Biggest advantage: whatever resources we open as a part of try block will be closed automatically. We are not required to close explicitly.</p>
<p>If you open database connection inside try — no problem — will be closed automatically. Finally block is not required to write. Complexity of programming reduced. Our work is performed by the syntax itself. Length of the code reduced. Readability improved.</p>
<h3>40:29 — Board: `try (BufferedReader br = ...)`</h3>
<p>Until 1.6 vs 1.7. After try, what did we take until 1.6? After try we opened the resource inside the block. In try-with-resources: after try, bracket open — try ( — this is try resource. Don’t think too much. R is nothing but resource: BufferedReader, database connection, network connection, FileWriter — any resource.</p>
<pre class='code'>import java.io.*;

class TryWithResources17 {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
        try (BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(&quot;input.txt&quot;))) {
            // use br based on our requirement
        } catch (IOException e) {
            // handling code
        }
    }
}</pre>
<p>input.txt — this total thing is the resource. Try with the resource. Brace open. BufferedReader already created. Use br based on our requirement. That’s all.</p>
<p>Once the control reaches end of try block, either normally or abnormally, either with exception or without exception, automatically br will be closed. We are not required to close.</p>
<p>Just write catch IOException. Can you tell: is finally required? Not required, because br will be closed automatically once control reaches end of try block.</p>
<h3>43:00 — Which practice is good? 1.7. New version = red carpet</h3>
<p>Which programming practice is good / easy to the programmer — first one or second one? Second. Easiness of programming by default will be improved with newer versions. That’s why if new version is there we have to give red carpet welcome. Most programmers are a bit rigid to accept new versions. New versions’ main purpose is to simplify programming complexity. If any new version is coming we have to celebrate like a festival — our life is going to be simplified.</p>
<p>Until 1.6 this much code. Suddenly in 1.7 length reduced. That is the advantage of try-with-resources.</p>
