<h3>1:44:23 — Extra word 1: exception propagation</h3>
<p>Keep writing: exception propagation. Simple enough.</p>
<p>Suppose I have `m1` method and I’m calling `m2` method. From m1 I’m calling m2. Inside m2, if an exception raised: System.out.println(10 / 0). Simple. Have you remembered default exception handling we covered.</p>
<p>Inside a method if an exception raised and if we are not handling that exception, then that exception object will be propagated to caller. Exception object will be propagated to caller by JVM. Then who is responsible to handle? Caller method is responsible to handle. This process is nothing but exception propagation.</p>
<p>He didn’t use this word before, but the concept you people are aware.</p>
<pre class='code'>class ExceptionPropagationDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        m1();
    }

    public static void m1() {
        m2(); // caller of m2
    }

    public static void m2() {
        System.out.println(10 / 0); // exception raised, not handled here
    }
}
// runtime: ArithmeticException  (propagated m2 -&gt; m1 -&gt; main -&gt; JVM default handler)</pre>
<h3>1:45:47 — Board dictation: exception propagation</h3>
<p>Inside a method if an exception raised and if we are not handling that exception, then exception object will be propagated to caller. Then caller method is responsible to handle exception. This process is called exception propagation.</p>
<pre class='code'>class ExceptionPropagationBoard {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // BOARD:
        // inside a method if an exception raised
        // and if we are not handling that exception
        // then exception object will be propagated to caller
        // then caller method is responsible to handle exception
        // this process is called exception propagation
        System.out.println(&quot;unhandled exception object -&gt; caller = exception propagation&quot;);
    }
}
// prints unhandled exception object -&gt; caller = exception propagation</pre>
<h3>1:47:31 — Extra word 2: rethrowing exception</h3>
<p>One more word. I have try block. Within the try, ArithmeticException raised: System.out.println(10 / 0). Catch ArithmeticException. Within that: `throw new NullPointerException()`.</p>
<p>Have you observed: actually raised exception is `ArithmeticException`, but we are throwing `NullPointerException`. This approach is nothing but rethrowing exception.</p>
<pre class='code'>class RethrowDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            System.out.println(10 / 0);
        } catch (ArithmeticException e) {
            throw new NullPointerException();
        }
    }
}
// runtime: NullPointerException</pre>
<h3>1:48:36 — When: convert one exception type to another (`web.xml` error page)</h3>
<p>When we can use this? To convert one exception to another exception type. Maybe for NullPointerException I configured some error page in the `web.xml`. If NullPointerException is going to come, that error page will come. Even for ArithmeticException also same error page I want to display — then convert this ArithmeticException into NullPointerException and throw NullPointerException.</p>
<p>To convert one exception to another exception type we can use this approach. This approach is rethrowing exception. Main advantage: one exception type to another exception type we will convert.</p>
<h3>1:49:21 — Board: rethrowing exception</h3>
<p>Keep writing: rethrowing exception. We can use this approach to convert one exception to another exception — one exception type to another exception type.</p>
<h3>1:50:12 — Board example</h3>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            System.out.println(10 / 0);
        } catch (ArithmeticException e) {
            throw new NullPointerException();
        }
    }
}
// runtime: NullPointerException</pre>
<p>That’s all. This is about rethrowing an exception.</p>
<h3>1:50:51 — Close: ~20 subtopics including 1.7</h3>
<p>That’s all these are various things what you people should aware related to exception handling. Almost around 20 subtopics we covered. Just go through all that. Then I’m sure definitely you can declare: yes we know, we are very perfect in the exception handling. Just refer all these things. That’s all the total things whatever there inside exception handling including 1.7 versions also. That’s all.</p>
<p>Video ends 1:51:30. He does not start a new chapter after rethrowing.</p>
<h2>Board recap he wants in the notebook</h2>
<p>Top 10 leftovers (this video):</p>
<p>JVM exceptions (1–6, raised automatically by JVM): ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException, NullPointerException, ClassCastException, StackOverflowError, NoClassDefFoundError, ExceptionInInitializerError (exam trap — new for most people).</p>
<p>1.7 try-with-resources: resources closed automatically at end of try (normal or abnormal). No explicit close(), finally not required. Until 1.6 finally is hero; from 1.7 finally is dummy / zero. Multiple resources separated by semicolon. Resources must be `AutoCloseable` (java.lang.AutoCloseable, 1.7, only close()). Resource refs are implicitly final — reassignment CE: auto-closeable resource br may not be assigned. From 1.7, try-with-resource without catch or finally is valid; until 1.6 try must have catch or finally.</p>
<p>1.7 multi-catch: catch (A | B e) — one catch, multiple types, same handling. Advantage: length down, readability up. No parent/child/same-type relation among alternatives. CE: Alternatives in a multi-catch statement cannot be related by subclassing.</p>
<p>Exception propagation: unhandled exception object propagated to caller; caller is responsible to handle.</p>
<p>Rethrowing: catch one type, throw new another type — convert one exception type to another (his web.xml error-page story).</p>
